# <%= current_page.data.title %>

The [secret expiry workflow](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/analytical-platform/blob/main/.github/workflows/secret-scan.yml) uses cross-account IAM access to run [the scanner script](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/analytical-platform/blob/main/scripts/secret-scan/check_secret_expiry.py). The script reports expiry dates recorded as tags on AWS Secrets Manager secrets; it does not retrieve or inspect secret values.

## What runs

The workflow is named **Secret expiry check** and is shceduled to run daily Monday to Friday:

1. Checks out the repository without persisting the checkout token.
2. Uses GitHub's OIDC identity to assume `github-actions-secret-check` in the management production account (`042130406152`).
3. Sets up Python 3.13 and installs `boto3`.
4. Runs the scanner. It checks Secrets Manager in `eu-west-1` and `eu-west-2` for each account listed in the script.
5. Writes a status table to the GitHub Actions run summary. For expired, critical, or warning results, it assumes `GlobalGitHubActionAccess`, retrieves the Slack webhook URL from Secrets Manager, and posts a Slack notification in the [#analytical-platform-engineering](https://moj.enterprise.slack.com/archives/C06NFN4FMNG) Slack channel.

The scanner uses the `analytical platform management production` role's credentials for that account and assumes `github-actions-secret-check` in each other account. This Terraform module creates those roles, their `secretsmanager:ListSecrets` policies, and the cross-account trust/access required by the scanner. The role in management production also has GitHub OIDC trust for this repository.

For a successful scan, the GitHub role must be able to list secrets in management production and assume the scanner role in every configured target account to be scanned. The target roles must trust the management production scanner role and allow `secretsmanager:ListSecrets`. Slack alerts additionally require the `GlobalGitHubActionAccess` role to read `arn:aws:secretsmanager:eu-west-1:042130406152:secret:secret-scan-webhook-url`.

## Statuses and alerts

The scanner compares each date with the current UTC date. The thresholds are inclusive as shown:

| Status | Meaning | Workflow handling |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `EXPIRED` | Expiry date is before today. | GitHub error annotation and Slack alert. |
| `CRITICAL` | Expires today or within the next 7 days. | GitHub warning annotation and Slack alert. |
| `WARNING` | Expires in 8 to 30 days. | GitHub warning annotation and Slack alert. |
| `OK` | Expires more than 30 days from today. | Included in the run summary; no Slack alert. |
| `INVALID` | `expiry-date` is present but is not a valid `YYYY-MM-DD` date. | GitHub warning annotation and run summary; no Slack alert. |
| `NOT SET` | `expiry-date` is present but empty or set to `none`, `null`, `n/a`, or `na` (case-insensitive). | Included in the run summary; no Slack alert. |

Only `EXPIRED`, `CRITICAL`, and `WARNING` counts trigger the workflow's Slack steps. An account credential or regional scan failure is logged as a GitHub Actions warning and the scanner continues; it does not create a expiry alert.

## Secret tags to scan

The scanner includes a secret only when it has a tag whose key is exactly `expiry-date`. Set the value to the expected expiry date in `YYYY-MM-DD` format, for example:

```hcl
tags = {
  expiry-date     = "2026-12-31"
  source-location = "service or location where the credential must be updated"
  rotation-policy = "any information on the rotation policy"
}
```

`source-location` is optional and is shown in the run summary to help identify where a credential needs updating; when absent, it is shown as `N/A`. A secret without an `expiry-date` tag is ignored entirely. A tagged secret with a blank or recognised "not set" value is included as `NOT SET`; other malformed dates are `INVALID`. The scanner reads secret names and tags through `ListSecrets`, not secret values.

To include a secret in the scan add the tag to the terraform for the secret as per the example above.

## Adding an AWS account to the scan

In the account to be scanned

Create the role `github-actions-secret-check` and associated policy as follows:

1. Add a scanner policy module in [iam-policies.tf](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/analytical-platform/blob/main/terraform/aws/analytical-platform/secret-scan/iam-policies.tf) for that provider, granting `secretsmanager:ListSecrets`.
2. Add a scanner role module in [iam-roles.tf](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/analytical-platform/blob/main/terraform/aws/analytical-platform/secret-scan/iam-roles.tf) for that provider. It must be named `github-actions-secret-check`, attach the scanner policy, and trust `arn:aws:iam::042130406152:role/github-actions-secret-check` for `sts:AssumeRole`.


The new account must be added both to the scanner's account list and to the Terraform module's cross-account IAM setup. For a typical additional target account:

1. Add its name, 12-digit account ID, and `role_name: "github-actions-secret-check"` to `ACCOUNTS` in [the scanner script](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/analytical-platform/blob/main/scripts/secret-scan/check_secret_expiry.py). `role_name: None` is only for the account whose credentials the workflow initially assumes `analytical platform management production`.
2. Add the account name and ID to `account_ids` in [terraform.tfvars](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/analytical-platform/blob/main/terraform/aws/analytical-platform/secret-scan/terraform.tfvars).
3. Add an aliased AWS provider for the account in [terraform.tf](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/analytical-platform/blob/main/terraform/aws/analytical-platform/secret-scan/terraform.tf), using the account's `GlobalGitHubActionAdmin` role, so Terraform can manage its scanner role and policy.
4. Add the new account's scanner role ARN to the `AllowAssumeRole` resources in [data.tf](https://github.com/ministryofjustice/analytical-platform/blob/main/terraform/aws/analytical-platform/secret-scan/data.tf), which defines the management production role's permission to assume target roles.
5. Apply this Terraform module, then run the workflow manually and verify the new account appears in the run summary. Confirm any secrets intended for monitoring have the required `expiry-date` tag.

The current account list and scanned regions are defined in the script's `ACCOUNTS` and `REGIONS` constants. If the new account needs additional regions, update `REGIONS` as well; all configured regions are checked for every listed account.

## Rotating the contents of the secret

How to update the source contents of the secret are beyond the scope of this document and hopefully the tags will give some clues on how to proceeed.

Once the the secret has been rotated the `expiry-date` will have to be updated in code.
